---
/**
 * Analysis — the second half of the lead offer (ADR-0001), at
 * `/services/statistics/`.
 *
 * The route says `statistics` because that is the word a Researcher searches
 * for and the architecture in the spec fixes it; the offer is **Analysis**, the
 * canonical term in `GLOSSARY.md`, and the copy uses that.
 *
 * Five things are named: study design, analysis, publication statistics,
 * reviewer response, and Coaching. Publication statistics — the whole of the
 * site's former lead offer — is one package here.
 *
 * This page carries the credit policy (ADR-0002) as one paragraph plus the
 * three cases decided in advance. The policy governs Analysis only. If the
 * paragraph is edited, ADR-0002 and the Chinese page must be edited with it.
 */
import ContactCallToAction from '../../components/ContactCallToAction.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Analysis for clinical research — Dr. Wei-Hung Pan"
  description="Study design, analysis, publication statistics, reviewer response and Coaching for clinical research groups. Fixed-scope packages, quoted per project."
>
  <h1>Your data is clean and there is no statistician on the team</h1>

  <p>
    The study is yours, the dataset is in order, and the method is the part you
    were never trained for. So the model gets chosen because the last paper in
    your field used it, the reviewer asks why, and the revision costs you four
    months you had not planned to spend.
  </p>

  <p>
    I run the statistics and hand back results you can defend: the analysis, the
    tables and figures as the journal wants them, the methods text, and the code
    that regenerates all of it from your dataset. I have worked with health and
    clinical data since 2012, and my own papers have been through this process in
    <em>Genome Medicine</em>, <em>Gastroenterology</em> and
    <em>Cellular and Molecular Gastroenterology and Hepatology</em> — so the
    reviewer's objection is one I have had to answer myself.
  </p>

  <h2>Study design, before the first patient</h2>

  <p>
    Endpoints, comparison, sample size and the analysis plan, agreed while they
    can still be changed. One or two sessions, and the cheapest thing on this
    page: no amount of statistics recovers a study that collected the wrong
    variable, and I would rather tell you that in March than next January.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and results</h2>

  <p>
    Your dataset in, and out comes the modelling the question actually calls for,
    with the assumptions checked and stated, the handling of missing data decided
    rather than defaulted, tables and figures to the journal's specification, a
    methods section drafted in publishable English, and reproducible code you
    keep. Where a reviewer later asks for a sensitivity analysis, the code is
    already set up to produce it.
  </p>

  <h2>Publication statistics</h2>

  <p>
    The whole statistical half of one paper, scoped as a package: design review
    of what has already been collected, the analysis, the results, the methods
    text, and the figures. For a Researcher who needs a paper in an SCI-indexed
    journal and needs the statistics in it to survive peer review rather than
    merely reach a p-value.
  </p>

  <h2>Reviewer response</h2>

  <p>
    Revise-and-resubmit, specifically the statistical comments: what the reviewer
    is actually objecting to, which objections are right, the reanalysis where
    one is right, and a point-by-point response written so an editor can see the
    matter has been settled. Bring the comments and the original analysis, mine
    or not.
  </p>

  <h2>Coaching: you run it, I teach you</h2>

  <p>
    One Researcher, their own data, their own analysis — and me beside it. We
    work through the design, the method and the code; you run every step; you
    leave able to explain and defend the choices to a reviewer, a committee or an
    examiner without me in the room. This is the offer for a thesis, for a first
    corresponding-author paper, and for anyone who would rather own the skill
    than rent it. It is also, under the policy below, the only honest offer where
    the work is being graded.
  </p>

  <h2>How credit is handled</h2>

  <p>
    <strong
      >If my name can appear in the output, I can run the Analysis. If the work
      is a graded assessment of your own competence, I teach you to run it
      instead.</strong
    > That is the whole rule, and it governs Analysis only — nobody is credited as
    the author of a data pipeline or a Workshop, so Engineering raises no question
    of authorship. Credit itself follows the ICMJE criteria and your target journal's
    policy: acknowledgement or co-authorship, as the contribution warrants. What the
    rule settles is the question before that one — whether I should be doing the work
    at all. It is published here because buying statistics should never put your paper
    or your standing at risk, and because checking it costs one question you can answer
    before you write to me.
  </p>

  <p>Three cases are decided in advance, so no engagement has to negotiate them:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A thesis assembled from published papers is fine.</strong> The
      competence was assessed when those papers were reviewed, and the thesis
      assembles work that already carries my name where I contributed to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A supervisor commissioning analysis for a paper is fine.</strong> The
      person buying is not the person being assessed, and the output is a paper
      that can credit me.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An engagement sold as Coaching stays Coaching.</strong> Where your
      own competence is what is being graded, I do not quietly take the keyboard
      because the deadline got close. That drift is the failure this rule exists
      to prevent and the hardest thing to notice from inside a friendly
      engagement, so I will say no on your behalf.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is bought</h2>

  <p>
    A design consult, the statistics for one paper, a round of reviewer response,
    a block of Coaching: each is a <strong>fixed-scope</strong> package, never an
    hourly rate. What you provide, what you receive and when it is finished are
    written down before any work starts.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are <strong>no published prices</strong>. The same paper can be a week
    or a quarter of work depending on the state of the data and the design, so a
    price list would be either dishonest or useless. Send me the research
    question, a description of the data and your deadline, and you get a written
    scope and a quote — itemised for a grant office if your funding needs that.
  </p>

  <h2>What I do not do</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ghostwriting.</strong> I draft the methods and the results, because
      that is the statistics. I do not write your paper.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work that is being graded.</strong> Where your own competence is
      what the assessment measures, the policy above applies: the analysis is not
      mine to run, and what I can sell you is Coaching.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Statistics chosen to produce an answer.</strong> I will not run
      tests until one of them is significant, and I will not drop cases to move a
      p-value. If the data do not support the claim, you hear it from me, which is
      cheaper than hearing it from a reviewer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An hour of someone glancing at your dataset.</strong> No hourly
      "quick look": either the question is worth scoping or it is a conversation
      you should not be paying for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Regulatory submission deliverables.</strong> Nothing feeding a
      filing to a regulator, and no analysis under a submission's validation
      requirements.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <ContactCallToAction
    locale="en"
    heading="Send me the research question"
    body="The question, what the data look like, the journal or the committee you are writing for, and your deadline. You get a written scope, a fixed price, and a straight answer on which of the five this is — including whether it should be Coaching."
    action="Request a quote"
  />
</BaseLayout>
